rows={1:(1.5,'Algebra',['AM–GM'],'Two direct AM–GM inequalities combine x⁴+y⁴≥2x²y² with 2x²y²+z²≥2√2xyz.'),2:(3,'Geometry',['Circumcentres','Right angles'],'The two auxiliary centres lie on opposite sides of AE, each seeing the chord at a right central angle; equal radii give the square.'),3:(4,'Combinatorics',['Graph theory','Shortest paths'],'Assign each town to a nearest city, breaking ties by a fixed city order. Every vertex on a chosen shortest route inherits the same nearest city and tie-break.'),4:(None,'Combinatorics',['Peg solitaire','Parity'],'The three-colour parity obstruction is verified, but the raw construction diagrams are corrupted and the full reduction to one or two pegs has not been checked.'),5:(2,'Number Theory',['Digits'],'Enumerate the five four-digit multiples of 1992 and choose a number differing from every one in every position, such as 2011.'),6:(3,'Geometry',['Equal chords','Reflection'],'Equal arcs identify the two base angles of the reflected triangle; congruence across the shared chord finishes.'),7:(4.5,'Algebra',['Monotonicity','Equations'],'Recognizing the sum t+⋯+t⁷ as strictly increasing even for negative arguments forces x=y; factorization then gives only 0 and −1.'),8:(5.5,'Combinatorics',['Tilings','Double counting'],'Boundary contact forces even side lengths. Counting the six corner incidences of each L-tromino against the maximum allowed at interior and boundary lattice points gives a deficit of two.'),9:(2.5,'Algebra',['AM–GM'],'The elementary inequality x²≥4(x−1), combined for x,y with AM–GM, gives the lower bound eight.'),10:(3,'Number Theory',['Primes','Pigeonhole principle'],'Every composite at most 1992 has a prime factor among the fourteen primes at most 43; pairwise coprimality makes fifteen composites impossible.'),11:(4.5,'Combinatorics',['Directed cycles','Construction'],'Following each occupant to the occupant of their intended seat produces a directed cycle that can be corrected at once. A star of oversold seats gives the sharp guarantee of one.'),12:(5,'Geometry',['Cyclic quadrilaterals','Angle chasing'],'Equal distances from O convert the crossing-chord angle into a cyclicity statement for YRSX; a final half-central-angle calculation gives the right angle.'),14:(3.5,'Geometry',['Excircles','Angle bisectors'],'Equal tangent lengths give AX=AY equal to the common semiperimeter; parallel angle bisectors make AW=AZ=AC, so the differences agree.'),15:(4.5,'Combinatorics',['Graph orientations','Vectors','Parity'],'An odd complete graph has a balanced orientation. For even colour-class sizes, remove the opposite-coloured corner pair, balance the remaining odd graphs, and use the board centroid to cancel the two stars.'),16:(6,'Combinatorics',['Graph theory','Double counting'],'Assuming diameter two forces all breadth-two neighbourhoods distinct and forbids triangles and quadrilaterals. Each vertex then lies on exactly eighteen pentagons, contradicting divisibility of the total incidence count by five.'),17:(2.5,'Algebra',['Trigonometry'],'Combine the shifted cosines into A cos x+B sin x; a nonzero sinusoid cannot have two roots in an interval of length π.'),18:(None,'Geometry',['Sphere','Power of a point'],'The source says the second plane merely intersects AB, but its proof requires the plane to contain AB. The stated claim is not justified; the intended stronger incidence condition needs original-source confirmation.'),19:(4,'Algebra',['Polynomials','Symmetric expressions'],'Separate x=y, then introduce s=x+y and t=xy. Subtracting the equations expresses t quadratically in s, and adding them leaves a cubic followed by a quadratic.'),20:(5.5,'Number Theory',['Digits','Modular arithmetic','Bounds'],'Equal digit length bounds k and k^(b−a)−1; reduction modulo nine restricts k to 3,6,9, and a leading/trailing-digit argument eliminates 6 and 9.'),22:(4,'Combinatorics',['Games','Vectors','Greedy strategy'],'Project along the total vector and always take a remaining vector of largest projection. Pair successive moves and compare squared norms using their equal perpendicular-component magnitudes.'),23:(4,'Number Theory',['Integer bounds','Factorisation'],'Writing the three excesses over d shows x−y−z is a positive integer; the identity d(x−y−z)=yz reduces the bound to an elementary positive expression with minimum eight.')}
statements={
3:r'A connected road network has $n$ distinguished cities and any number of towns. Partition all vertices into $n$ regions, each containing exactly one city, so that every town shares a region with every vertex of at least one route to its city using the fewest possible towns.',
7:r'Find all real pairs $(x,y)$ satisfying $$(1+x)(1+x^2)(1+x^4)=1+y^7,\qquad(1+y)(1+y^2)(1+y^4)=1+x^7.$$',
8:r'Can a rectangle of unit cells be tiled by L-trominoes so that no two tiles together form a rectangle and no lattice vertex belongs to four tiles? Determine every possible pair of side lengths.',
11:r'A cinema has $n$ rows and $m$ columns. It issues $mn$ tickets, allowing repeated seat labels. Suppose everyone can be seated with either the correct row or the correct column. Prove that such a seating can also give at least one person their exact seat, and determine the largest universally guaranteed number of exact seats.',
12:r'Two circles meet at $O,X$. A circle centred at $O$ cuts the first at $Q,R$ and the second at $P,S$. If $Y=PR\cap QS$ exists and differs from $X$, prove $XY\perp OX$.',
15:r'Exactly half the cells of a $2m\times n$ board are black. The two cells at opposite ends of a main diagonal have different colours. Join every pair of same-coloured cell centres. Prove that these segments can be oriented so that the sum of all resulting vectors is zero.',
16:r'In a simple graph with $17$ vertices, every vertex has degree $4$. Prove that some pair of vertices is neither adjacent nor joined to a common neighbour.',
19:r'Assume an algorithm is available for finding all real roots of any cubic polynomial. Explain how to find all real solutions of $x=p(y)$ and $y=p(x)$, where $p$ is a given real cubic.',
20:r'For which integers $k>1$ can $k^a+1$ and $k^b+1$, with distinct positive integers $a,b$, be reversals of one another in decimal notation?',
22:r'Two players alternately choose from $1992$ given plane vectors, taking all of them. Each player’s score is the length of the sum of their chosen vectors. Can the first player guarantee at least a draw?',
23:r'Positive integers satisfy $a>b>c>d$ and $ad=bc$. Prove that $(a-d)^2\ge4d+8$.'}
solutions={
3:r'Order the cities. Assign each town to a city at minimum road distance, choosing the first city in that order when necessary. Take a shortest route from a town $T$ to its assigned city $C$. Any intermediate town $U$ cannot be closer to a different city, since that would shorten the route from $T$. Nor can it have an equally close earlier city, since that would win the tie-break for $T$. Thus every intermediate town is assigned to $C$. No intermediate vertex is a city, since that would be a closer city. The resulting regions have the required property.',
7:r'Put $g(t)=t+t^2+\cdots+t^7$. It is strictly increasing for $t\ge0$. For $u>0$, $$g(-u)=-\frac{u+u^8}{1+u}=-\frac{u}{1+u}(1+u^7),$$ which is strictly decreasing as a function of $u$; hence $g$ is also strictly increasing on the negative axis, and therefore on all of $\mathbb R$. The equations are $g(x)=y^7$ and $g(y)=x^7$, so strict monotonicity forces $x=y$. They then reduce to $$x+x^2+\cdots+x^6=x(1+x)(1+x^2+x^4)=0.$$ Thus $(x,y)=(0,0)$ or $(-1,-1)$, and both work.',
11:r'Direct an arrow from each person to the person currently occupying their ticketed seat. Every vertex has one outgoing arrow, so there is a directed cycle. Move each person on that cycle to their ticketed seat; all other people stay put. This keeps everyone in an allowed row or column and makes at least one seat exact. To show sharpness, issue $m+n-1$ tickets for seat $(1,1)$ and $m-1$ tickets for each seat $(i,1)$ with $2\le i\le n$. The first group must occupy the union of the first row and first column. Each other group fills the remaining cells of its row. Exactly one person has their ticketed seat. Thus the sharp guarantee is one.',
15:r'For any odd number of points, orient the complete graph as a regular tournament, so every point has equal indegree and outdegree. The vector sum is then zero because each position vector has coefficient zero. If $mn$ is odd, apply this separately to each colour. If $mn$ is even, remove the black corner centre $B$ and opposite white corner centre $W$. Balance each remaining odd colour class, and direct the remaining black edges out of $B$ and white edges out of $W$. Their sum is the sum of all cell-centre vectors minus $mn(B+W)$, which is zero because the board’s centre is $(B+W)/2$.',
16:r'Suppose every pair has distance at most two. From any vertex $v$, the four neighbours and their three further neighbours must be sixteen distinct vertices. Consequently the graph has no triangles or quadrilaterals. There are $34$ edges in total. Of these, four join $v$ to its neighbours and twelve join those neighbours to the remaining twelve vertices; the other eighteen join different branches of this neighbourhood. Each such edge gives one pentagon through $v$, and every pentagon through $v$ arises this way. Hence every vertex lies on eighteen pentagons. Counting vertex–pentagon incidences would make the number of pentagons $17\cdot18/5$, not an integer. This contradiction proves the claim.',
19:r'Write $p(z)=az^3+bz^2+cz+d$, with $a\ne0$. Solutions with $x=y$ are roots of $p(x)-x=0$. For $x\ne y$, set $s=x+y$ and $t=xy$. Subtracting the equations gives $$a(s^2-t)+bs+c+1=0,$$ so $t=s^2+(bs+c+1)/a$. Adding gives $$s=a(s^3-3st)+b(s^2-2t)+cs+2d.$$ Substitution yields a cubic in $s$ with leading coefficient $-2a$. Solve it, recover $t$, then solve $z^2-sz+t=0$. Retain its distinct real roots only when the resulting ordered pairs satisfy the original equations. This finite filtering also removes any extraneous candidates.',
22:r'Let $S$ be the total vector. If $S=0$, the players’ sums are opposites, so every play draws. Otherwise use the unit vector $e=S/|S|$. The first player always chooses a remaining vector with largest projection onto $e$. Each first-player choice has projection at least that of the following second-player choice. Hence the final sums $A,B$ satisfy $(A-B)\cdot e\ge0$. Since $A+B=S$, $$|A|^2-|B|^2=(A-B)\cdot S\ge0.$$ Thus the first player cannot lose.',
23:r'Write $a=d+x$, $b=d+y$, $c=d+z$, where $x>y>z\ge1$. The product condition becomes $d(x-y-z)=yz$. Thus $q=x-y-z$ is a positive integer and $d=yz/q$. Therefore $$x^2-4d=(y+z+q)^2-\frac{4yz}{q}\ge(y-z)^2+2q(y+z)+q^2\ge1+6+1=8,$$ as required.'}
